      "dom_id": "question-26595",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The point  $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,1-1)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,0)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(0))=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,0)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,0-1)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=1\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=0.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)-1 \\\\  & =-0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$",
